{"task_id": "Mbpp/9", "solution": "def find_Rotations(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the minimum number of rotations (greater than 0) required to get the same string.\n    Uses the Knuth-Morris-Pratt failure function to determine the string's smallest period.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # Compute the Longest Proper Prefix which is also a Suffix (LPS) array.\n    # This array helps find the smallest repeating unit within the string.\n    lps = [0] * n\n    length = 0  # Length of the previous longest prefix suffix\n    i = 1\n    while i < n:\n        if s[i] == s[length]:\n            length += 1\n            lps[i] = length\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            if length != 0:\n                length = lps[length - 1]\n            else:\n                lps[i] = 0\n                i += 1\n    \n    # The length of the longest proper prefix of the string that is also a suffix.\n    l = lps[n - 1]\n    \n    # The smallest period of the string is n - l.\n    # If the string length is divisible by this period, it's the minimal rotations.\n    # Otherwise, the string itself is the smallest period.\n    period = n - l\n    \n    if n % period == 0:\n        return period\n    else:\n        return n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/3", "solution": "def is_not_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies if a given integer is a non-prime number.\n    Returns True for non-prime numbers and False for prime numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 2:\n        return True  # Numbers less than 2 are non-prime (by definition or convention)\n    if n == 2:\n        return False  # 2 is prime\n    \n    # Check for divisors from 2 up to the square root of n\n    # If any divisor is found, n is non-prime\n    i = 2\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return True\n        i += 1\n    return False # No divisors found, so n is prime"}
