{"task_id": "Mbpp/84", "solution": "def sequence(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the nth number in the Newman-Conway sequence using dynamic programming.\n    The sequence is defined by P(1) = 1, P(2) = 1, and P(n) = P(P(n-1)) + P(P(n-2)) for n > 2.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        # Assuming n will always be a positive integer based on typical sequence definitions.\n        # Per instructions, no explicit error handling for invalid input n.\n        return 0 # Or some other default, but the sequence is for n >= 1\n\n    if n == 1 or n == 2:\n        return 1\n\n    # Initialize a list to store sequence terms, using 1-based indexing.\n    # dp[i] will store the i-th term P(i).\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    dp[1] = 1\n    dp[2] = 1\n\n    # Calculate terms from P(3) up to P(n)\n    for i in range(3, n + 1):\n        # Apply the recurrence relation: P(i) = P(P(i-1)) + P(P(i-2))\n        # The values P(i-1) and P(i-2) are used as indices for the dp array.\n        # This requires that P(k) >= 1 and P(k) <= k for all k.\n        # This property holds for the Newman-Conway sequence.\n        dp[i] = dp[dp[i-1]] + dp[dp[i-2]]\n\n    return dp[n]"}
