{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "def next_Perfect_Square(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the next perfect square greater than a given number.\n    Returns 0 for negative inputs as per FR1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Calculate the integer square root of n\n    # If n is a perfect square, sqrt_n will be its root.\n    # If n is not a perfect square, sqrt_n will be floor(sqrt(n)).\n    sqrt_n = int(n**0.5)\n    \n    # If n is a perfect square, say n = k*k, then the next perfect square is (k+1)*(k+1).\n    # If n is not a perfect square, say k*k < n < (k+1)*(k+1), then the next perfect square is (k+1)*(k+1).\n    # In both cases, the root of the next perfect square is ceil(sqrt(n+1)) if n is not a perfect square\n    # Or sqrt(n)+1 if n is a perfect square.\n    # A simpler way is to find ceil(sqrt(n+1)) which means (floor(sqrt(n)) + 1) if n is a perfect square\n    # or (floor(sqrt(n)) + 1) if n is not a perfect square.\n    # Essentially, the root of the next perfect square is floor(sqrt(n)) + 1.\n    next_root = sqrt_n + 1\n    \n    return next_root * next_root"}
