{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "def largest_subset(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the size of the largest subset of numbers where every pair is divisible.\n    This uses a dynamic programming approach after sorting the input list.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort the numbers to simplify divisibility checks and DP state transitions.\n    nums_sorted = sorted(nums)\n\n    # dp[i] will store the size of the largest divisible subset ending with nums_sorted[i].\n    dp = [1] * n\n\n    # Iterate through each number to calculate the largest divisible subset ending at that number.\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If nums_sorted[i] is divisible by nums_sorted[j], it can extend the subset ending at j.\n            if nums_sorted[i] % nums_sorted[j] == 0:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n\n    # The maximum value in the dp array is the size of the largest divisible subset.\n    return max(dp)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "def get_median(list1, list2, size):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the median of two sorted lists of the same size.\n    The median is the average of the two middle elements from the combined sorted sequence.\n    \"\"\"\n    i = 0\n    j = 0\n    num1 = -1  # Stores the element at index (size - 1)\n    num2 = -1  # Stores the element at index size\n\n    # Iterate size + 1 times to find the two middle elements (at indices size-1 and size).\n    for _ in range(size + 1):\n        num1 = num2  # Shift num2 to num1, preparing for the next element\n\n        if i < size and j < size:\n            if list1[i] <= list2[j]:\n                num2 = list1[i]\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                num2 = list2[j]\n                j += 1\n        elif i < size:  # list2 is exhausted, take from list1\n            num2 = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        elif j < size:  # list1 is exhausted, take from list2\n            num2 = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n    \n    return (num1 + num2) / 2.0"}
